// Program arguments.
//
// `args()` is a builtin, in scope in every file, so this module documents the
// name rather than defining it. It answers the process's user arguments as a
// fresh owned `[]string`:
//
//     let arguments = args();
//     let path = switch arguments[0] {
//         case Some(value) { value }
//         case None { "" }
//     };
//     arguments.free();
//
//     if path.len() == 0 {
//         println("usage: mytool <path>");
//         return;
//     }
//     print(path);
//
// The executable path is excluded, so `arguments[0]` is the first argument
// after `yashy run --`. An invocation with no arguments yields an empty slice,
// not an error. `yashy run main.ya -- notes.txt` makes `arguments[0]` the
// string `notes.txt`.
//
// Ownership: every element is an owned string and the slice owns one reference
// to each; free the slice with `.free()` (which releases the elements too).
// Indexing is checked and yields `Option[string]`, so read an element after
// checking `.len()` or match the `Option`. The string bound from the `Option`
// is retained, so it stays valid after the slice is freed.
